@meta
id: 31-10
title: The LSP client is already there
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: nvim-lsp, lua-config
par: 108
minutes: 8
vscode: installing a language extension and never thinking about it again
open: lsp.lua

@brief
This is the change that actually moves people. Neovim has a Language
Server Protocol client built into the editor. Not a plugin — part of the
binary, documented at `:h lsp`, maintained with the core.

Be precise about what that means, because the marketing blurs it:

- **You still need the server.** The client speaks the protocol; something
  has to answer. For TypeScript that is `typescript-language-server`,
  installed with npm. Neovim does not ship servers and never will.
- **You no longer need a plugin to connect them.** From 0.11, two
  functions do it: `vim.lsp.config('name', { ... })` describes a server and
  `vim.lsp.enable('name')` turns it on for the filetypes it claims.
  `nvim-lspconfig` is still worth having — it is a library of ready-made
  configs for 300 servers — but it is a convenience now, not a requirement.
- **`root_markers` is how the server finds your project.** The client walks
  up from the current file until it sees one of these names, and that
  directory becomes the workspace root. Get this wrong and the server
  starts in your home directory and reports nonsense.

What you get once it attaches: real go-to-definition that understands
types rather than matching a name in an index, rename that is correct
across a project, diagnostics as you type, hover documentation, and
completion. Chapter 30's ctags index does the first of those honestly and
the others not at all — that is the difference, and it is a big one on a
large TypeScript codebase.

0.11 also ships default mappings, so an attached buffer gets `grn`
(rename), `gra` (code action), `grr` (references), `gri`
(implementation), `gO` (document symbols) and `K` (hover) without you
mapping anything.

Two jobs in the file: give the server its root markers —
`tsconfig.json`, `package.json`, `.git`, in that order — and turn it on at
the bottom of the file.

Graded in vim. Nothing here runs: no Neovim, no language server, no npm.

@keys
| `vim.lsp.config(name, opts)` | describe a server |
| `vim.lsp.enable(name)` | start it for its filetypes |
| `ci{` | change inside the braces |

@hints
The markers are a Lua list of strings, in the same style as `filetypes`
just above it. The second job is one line on its own at the end of the
file, separated by a blank line.
--
`ci{` works on the enclosing braces, so put the cursor on the empty pair
first — `f{` gets you there in two keys.
--
`/root_markers` and Enter, `f{`, `ci{`, type the list. Then `G`, `o`,
Enter for the blank line, and `vim.lsp.enable('ts_ls')`.

@solution
    /root_markers<Enter> f{ ci{ 'tsconfig.json', 'package.json', '.git' <Esc>
    G o <Enter> vim.lsp.enable('ts_ls') <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Around ninety keystrokes. `f{` before `ci{` is not optional: unlike `ci'`,
which looks forward on the line, `ci{` works outwards from the cursor and
would have taken the whole argument table of `vim.lsp.config` instead. The
shape is worth memorising because every
server you ever add is the same four fields: `cmd`, `filetypes`,
`root_markers`, and optionally `settings` for the server's own options.

Where people get stuck, in order of frequency:

1. The server binary is not on `$PATH`. `:checkhealth vim.lsp` says so in
   plain English. `mason.nvim` exists to install servers from inside
   Neovim, and is the usual answer if you do not want a global npm.
2. The root marker list is too narrow, so a monorepo package resolves to
   the repo root and every import is unresolved.
3. Nothing attached at all, because the filetype does not match. `:LspInfo`
   in older setups, `:checkhealth` now.

And the thing to keep in perspective: none of this changes how you edit.
It changes what the editor knows. `gd`, `K` and a rename are three keys
either way — the question is whether the answer comes from a text index or
from a compiler.

@start lsp.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/config/lsp.lua
-- Neovim 0.11+, no lspconfig plugin: the built-in client is enough.

vim.lsp.config('ts_ls', {
  cmd = { 'typescript-language-server', '--stdio' },
  filetypes = { 'typescript', 'typescriptreact' },
  root_markers = {},
})

@expected lsp.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/lua/config/lsp.lua
-- Neovim 0.11+, no lspconfig plugin: the built-in client is enough.

vim.lsp.config('ts_ls', {
  cmd = { 'typescript-language-server', '--stdio' },
  filetypes = { 'typescript', 'typescriptreact' },
  root_markers = { 'tsconfig.json', 'package.json', '.git' },
})

vim.lsp.enable('ts_ls')
